{"term":"inspire","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:f9c6a02243b595a9b5","definition":"To infuse into the mind; to communicate to the spirit; to convey, as by a divine or supernatural influence; to disclose preternaturally; to produce in, as by inspiration.","example":"Dawning day new comfort hath inspired.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["beghast"],"antonyms":["expire"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":["awe-inspiring","bio-inspired","inspirable","inspirer","inspirometer","misinspire","philatelically inspired","reinspire","uninspire"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inspire#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:92a2bd5bd50eecb9a0","definition":"To infuse into; to affect, as with a superior or supernatural influence; to fill with what animates, enlivens or exalts; to communicate inspiration to.","example":"Elders should inspire children with sentiments of virtue.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["beghast"],"antonyms":["expire"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":["awe-inspiring","bio-inspired","inspirable","inspirer","inspirometer","misinspire","philatelically inspired","reinspire","uninspire"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inspire#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:9b3b064774983e7b62","definition":"To draw in by the operation of breathing; to inhale.","example":"By means of those sulfurous coal smokes the lungs are as it were stifled and extremely oppressed, whereby they are forced to inspire and expire the air with difficulty.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["beghast"],"antonyms":["expire"],"collocations":[],"usage":"ambitransitive","register":"ambitransitive","wordFamily":["awe-inspiring","bio-inspired","inspirable","inspirer","inspirometer","misinspire","philatelically inspired","reinspire","unin